# Kafka Streams starter

This project declares `raw_orders -> eligible_orders -> fraud_app`.
The two topics and the SQL runner are managed by streamt. `fraud_app` records
a custom application's input and expected columns; streamt does not deploy,
run, or monitor its code. There is no producer application in this starter.

The supported deployment path is create/no-op only. Changing a running model
is blocked pending replacement/recovery support. The runtime-level update
experiment does not establish a supported CLI update workflow.

## Inspect the topology offline

```bash
streamt validate --strict
streamt lineage
streamt compile --dry-run
streamt plan --offline
```

These commands do not require running infrastructure. The offline plan assumes
managed resources are absent; it is not a live diff. Validation checks declared
contracts and references, not broker contents or whether `fraud_app` is running.

## Create the managed resources

The runner image must already exist on your local Docker daemon. To build the
packaged runner explicitly, run `streamt -o json runtime build`, then copy its
`data.image` ID into `runtime.kafka_streams.image`.

Before a live plan/apply, provide a Kafka broker that supports transactions,
the configured local Docker bridge network, and matching advertised broker
addresses. `runtime.kafka.bootstrap_servers` is reachable from your CLI;
`bootstrap_servers_internal` is reachable from the runner container. Inside
that container, `localhost` is the runner itself. The default `broker:19092`
requires your own network/broker configuration; init does not create either.
The default Docker `bridge` does not provide service-name DNS. Use an existing
user-defined bridge network when your broker address uses its container name.

The sample uses one partition and replication factor one for local testing.
These topic settings are not a production recommendation. Review the image,
network, broker addresses, and `initial_offset` in `stream_project.yml` before:

```bash
streamt plan --out reviewed-plan.json
streamt apply --plan reviewed-plan.json
streamt status --health
streamt plan
```

The first apply creates the input/output topics and one runner container. An
unchanged second plan should be a no-op. Creation initializes source offsets
explicitly from the chosen earliest/latest policy; it does not reset an
existing group. The runner itself uses `auto.offset.reset=none`.

Authentication belongs in runtime configuration through environment references,
not SQL, JSON plans, or sample records. This starter uses plaintext local Kafka.
TLS/SASL broker acceptance and production hardening remain separate work.

## Produce and read a sample

`sample_events.jsonl` contains four raw UTF-8 JSON objects. With
[Kafka's CLI tools](https://kafka.apache.org/43/getting-started/quickstart/) on
your PATH, run these commands against the host-facing broker address
configured in your project (replace `localhost:9092` if needed):

```bash
kafka-console-producer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic {{ raw_topic }} < sample_events.jsonl
kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic {{ output_topic }} --from-beginning --max-messages 1 \
  --timeout-ms 10000 --consumer-property isolation.level=read_committed
```

For this fresh input, the expected output is one JSON object:

```json
{"id":"large-paid","amount":150}
```

The smaller paid order, unpaid order, and null amount do not pass the predicate.
Repeating production sends new records; it is not a deduplicated seed command.
The runner preserves Kafka keys, including null keys. These CLI sample records
have null keys. Consumers should use `read_committed` to read committed output.

Values must contain exactly `id`, `amount`, and `paid`. Missing/extra fields,
wrong types, malformed JSON, and integers outside signed 64-bit range stop the
runner. Nullable `amount` must be explicit JSON null, not an omitted property.
Tombstones are dropped. This is append-stream behavior, not an upsert table.

## Absorb an existing source

Do not declare an existing topic as `raw_orders` and assume streamt will adopt
it. In a new project, remove the `raw_orders` managed model, then preview and
import the exact existing topic:

```bash
streamt import --include existing.orders.v1 --no-schemas --dry-run
streamt import --include existing.orders.v1 --no-schemas
```

Review the imported source's name and its `ownership.mode: external`. Import
without schemas cannot infer the record fields. Add the matching raw JSON
columns to that source declaration:

```yaml
columns:
  - {name: id, type: STRING, required: true}
  - {name: amount, type: BIGINT, required: false}
  - {name: paid, type: BOOLEAN, required: true}
```

Change the SQL input from `{% raw %}{{ ref("raw_orders") }}{% endraw %}` to
`{% raw %}{{ source("<imported_source_name>") }}{% endraw %}`. Validate and review a live
plan before applying the new consumer. The source stays external: streamt does
not create, adopt, reconfigure, seed, or continuously reconcile it. Runner
preflight still verifies the topic identity and usable offsets needed to run
the managed SQL consumer. Only its output topic and SQL runner are created.

Keep the custom application's declared consumed columns aligned with the SQL
output. Rename/remove a projected column and offline validation can identify
the affected declaration before any deployment. Fixing that declaration does
not deploy the application's code.
